    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d267">

                <pb facs="0319" n="254" sameAs="frus:frus1936v03/pg254"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">765.84/4236: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister Resident in Ethiopia</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Engert</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Addis
                                Ababa</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-05-02T09:00:00+02:35">May 2, 1936—9 a.m.</date><lb/>[Received
                        3:15 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>261. Apparently Government left during the night. Empress with daughters and
                    Prince Makonnen by train for Djibouti understand en route to Jerusalem.
                    Emperor’s destination unknown. Spencer<anchor n="18" xml:id="d267-f1"/> and
                        Lambies<anchor n="19" xml:id="d267-f2"/> did not accompany the
                    Government.</p>
                <p>As soon as news of the departure of Government became known looting began in town
                    which is continuing. There is a great deal of firing but mostly in the air. I
                    shall await developments in the Legation for the present.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Engert</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d267-f1" n="18" xml:id="d267-f1-content">
                <p>John H. Spencer, an American citizen who was Adviser to the Ethiopian Ministry of
                    Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d267-f2" n="19" xml:id="d267-f2-content">
                <p>Dr. and Mrs. T. A. Lambie. Dr. Lambie was Executive Secretary of the Ethiopian
                    Bed Cross.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
